"<main class=\"wrap\">\n  <section class=\"learn-top\">\n    <h1>What are access keys?</h1>\n    <p>A plain-words guide to how apps and agents get spending power on a Tempo account, and how that power can outlast the thing that was given it.</p>\n  </section>\n\n  <div class=\"learn\">\n    <nav class=\"toc\" aria-label=\"On this page\"><ol id=\"toc\"><span class=\"mark-ind\" id=\"tocmark\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span>\n      <li><a href=\"#what\">A second key, with rules</a></li>\n      <li><a href=\"#limits\">What a key can be limited to</a></li>\n      <li><a href=\"#admin\">Admin keys</a></li>\n      <li><a href=\"#leftover\">The leftover key</a></li>\n      <li><a href=\"#count\">How many keys are out there</a></li>\n      <li><a href=\"#check\">Check your account</a></li>\n    </ol></nav>\n\n    <article>\n      <section id=\"what\">\n        <h2>A second key, with rules</h2>\n        <p>Your Tempo account has one main key: the one you sign with. An access key is an extra key you add so an app, a card or an AI agent can make payments for you without holding your main key.</p>\n        <p>Each access key carries its own rules. The network checks those rules on every payment, so a key can't spend more, or pay someone else, just because the app holding it wants to.</p>\n        <p class=\"spec\">Defined in Tempo's account keychain specification, <a href=\"https://github.com/tempoxyz/tempo/blob/main/tips/tip-1011.md\">TIP-1011</a>.</p>\n      </section>\n\n      <section id=\"limits\">\n        <h2>What a key can be limited to</h2>\n        <p>Caps and expiry can be left unrestricted. An empty recipient list permits any recipient. A scoped key with no allowed contracts can call nothing.</p>\n        <div class=\"anat\" id=\"anat\">\n          <div class=\"r\"><b>Spending limit</b><span>How much it can spend, once or per period.</span><span class=\"ex\" data-a=\"3 AlphaUSD, once\" data-b=\"No cap\">3 AlphaUSD, once</span></div>\n          <div class=\"r\"><b>Who it can pay</b><span>A list of addresses it's allowed to send to.</span><span class=\"ex\" data-a=\"1 address\" data-b=\"Anyone\">1 address</span></div>\n          <div class=\"r\"><b>What it can call</b><span>Which contracts and functions it may use.</span><span class=\"ex\" data-a=\"transfer only\" data-b=\"Any function\">transfer only</span></div>\n          <div class=\"r\"><b>When it ends</b><span>An expiry date, after which it can't spend.</span><span class=\"ex\" data-a=\"One year\" data-b=\"Never\">One year</span></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"flip\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Example key\"><button aria-pressed=\"true\" data-k=\"a\">A careful key</button><button aria-pressed=\"false\" data-k=\"b\">A risky key</button></div>\n      </section>\n\n      <section id=\"admin\">\n        <h2>Admin keys</h2>\n        <p>Some keys are admin keys. On top of spending, an admin key can create new access keys on your account and revoke them. That's useful for an agent that manages its own sessions, but it means keys can appear on your account that you never made yourself.</p>\n        <p class=\"spec\">Added in <a href=\"https://github.com/tempoxyz/tempo/blob/main/tips/tip-1049.md\">TIP-1049</a>.</p>\n      </section>\n\n      <section id=\"leftover\">\n        <h2>The leftover key</h2>\n        <p>When you revoke an admin key, the keys it created stay active. Tempo's specification says so directly, and the chain keeps no record of which admin created which key. This illustration shows the testnet case:</p>\n        <div class=\"try\"><p class=\"fine\" style=\"color:var(--mint)\">Seeded illustration. No transaction is sent.</p>\n          <div class=\"tree\" id=\"tree\">\n            <div class=\"tn\">Your account</div><div class=\"tw\"></div>\n            <div class=\"tn\" id=\"tAdmin\">Admin key <span class=\"state\" id=\"tAdminS\">Active</span></div><div class=\"tw\" id=\"tw2\"></div>\n            <div class=\"tn\" id=\"tChild\">Child key <span class=\"state\" id=\"tChildS\">Active</span></div>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"say\" id=\"tsay\" aria-live=\"polite\">The agent holds the admin key. It made the child key.</p>\n          <div class=\"btns\"><button class=\"btn btn-cut\" id=\"tgo\">Cut admin access</button><button class=\"btn btn-line\" id=\"treset\" hidden>Reset</button></div>\n        </div>\n        <p class=\"muted\">The child key keeps its limits, but nothing on the account shows it's left over. Keycut lists every key from the address alone so you can find it.</p>\n      </section>\n\n      <section id=\"count\">\n        <h2>How many keys are out there</h2>\n        <p class=\"facts\" id=\"facts\">{{STATS}}</p>\n        <p class=\"muted\">{{DETAIL}} The admin-and-child case was verified on testnet.</p>\n      </section>\n\n      <section id=\"check\">\n        <div class=\"end\">\n          <h2>Check your account</h2>\n          <p class=\"muted\">Paste an address. It's read-only and needs no wallet.</p>\n          <form class=\"search\" action=\"/testnet/0xE5CB1Dd82e60Df2A1cd6b58dF218aea7d535521A\"><label class=\"sr\" for=\"a2\">Account address</label><input id=\"a2\" name=\"address\" placeholder=\"Paste a Tempo account address\"><button class=\"btn btn-cut\">Find keys</button></form>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n    </article>\n  </div>\n</main>"